At that time, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, “Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?”
Then Jesus called a little child to him, and stood him in the midst of them,
And He said: “Truly I say to you, unless you change and become like children, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven.”
Therefore, whoever humbles himself like this little child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
“And whoever welcomes a child like this in my name welcomes me.” Warning against stumbling blocks.
And whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to sin, it would be better for him if a millstone were hung around his neck and he were drowned in the sea!
Woe to the world for these stumbles that are bound to come, but woe to those who cause them!
Therefore, if your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away! It is better to enter eternal life with one hand or one foot than to be thrown into eternal fire with two hands or two feet.
And if your eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and cast it from you! For it is better for you to enter life with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the hell of fire.” The Lost Sheep
And Jesus said: “Take care not to despise one of these little ones. For I tell you that their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father.”
For the Son of Man came to save the lost.
So what do you say about a man who has a hundred sheep, and one strays? Won't he leave the ninety-nine on the mountain and go look for the lost one?
I tell you the truth, when he finds it, he will be happier about it than about the ninety-nine sheep which had not strayed.
So your Father who is in heaven does not want any of these humble disciples of mine to be lost. Reconcile with your brother.
If your brother wrongs you, go to him and speak with him in private. If he listens to you, you will have won back your brother.
But if he will not listen to you, take one or two others with you, so that the matter may be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses. #two or three witnesses. 16:18 See Deuteronomy 19:15.
If he refuses to listen to them, tell the church. If he refuses to listen to the church, then treat him as you would a pagan and a tax collector.
“Truly I tell you, whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”
And I tell you truly, if two of you on earth agree about anything they ask for, it will be done for them by my Father in heaven.
"For where two or three gather in my name, there am I among them.” Limitless forgiveness."
Then Peter came to Jesus and said to Him, “Lord, how often shall I forgive my brother who sins against me? Up to seven times?”
Then Jesus told him, “Not to seven times only, but seventy times seven! #seventy … times. 22:18 That is, without limit.”
Therefore, the kingdom of heaven can be likened to a king who decided to settle accounts with his servants.
And as he began to settle his accounts, a man indebted to him with a very large sum was brought to him. #A very large sum. 24:18 Literally: “Ten thousand weights or *qintars*, and this equals approximately 300,000 kilograms of the coins used at that time.”
And as he did not possess anything with which to settle the debt, the man decided to sell himself, his wife, and his children, along with everything he owned, and to use the proceeds to pay off the debt.
Then the servant fell on his knees before the king and pleaded with him, saying: ‘Be patient with me, and I will pay you back all the debt.’
So the master had compassion on him, cancelled his debt in full, and let him go.
And as he was going along, he met one of his fellow slaves, who owed him a small amount. So he seized him by the throat and began to choke him, saying, “Pay me what you owe.”
And the servant prostrated on his knees before Him, and pleaded with Him, saying: ‹Be patient with me, and I will pay what is due.›
But he refused, rather he took him and threw him in prison until he pays all his debt.
And when the other slaves saw what had happened, they were very sad, and went to tell their master everything that had happened.
So his master called him and said to him, “You wicked servant, should I not have had mercy on you and forgiven all your debt because you begged me?”
Shouldn't you have had mercy on your servant and companion, as I had mercy on you as well?
And his master was very angry, and handed him over to be punished until he paid all his debt.
So will your heavenly Father treat you, unless each of you forgives his brother from his heart.
Union in marriage
And after Jesus had finished saying these things, he left the region of Galilee and went to the region of Judea across the Jordan River.
And large crowds followed Him, and He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to him to test him, asking, “Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?”
Then Jesus answered, “Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator ‘made them male and female’? #creation … and female. 4:19 From Genesis 1:27, 5:2.
Then he said, “For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and the two will become one flesh.” #leave … one. 5:19 From Genesis 2:24.
And so they shall no longer be two, but one. Therefore what God has joined together, let no one separate.
So they said to Him, "Why then did Moses command to give a certificate of divorce, #certificate of divorce. 7:19 See Deuteronomy 24:1, and then to divorce?"
And Jesus answered them, “Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because your hearts were hard. But it was not this way from the beginning.”
Therefore I tell you that anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another commits adultery.”
His disciples said to him, “If this is the case between a husband and his wife, it is better not to marry!”
Then Jesus said to them, “Not everyone can accept this teaching, but only those to whom God has given it.”
There are men who cannot marry because they were born without the ability to marry. And there are men who cannot marry because people have deprived them of the ability to marry. And there are men who have chosen not to marry for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. Whoever can accept this teaching should accept it.” Jesus welcomes the children.
Then they brought little children to Him, so that He might touch them; and the disciples rebuked those who brought them.
Then Jesus said, “Let the little children come to me, and do not forbid them, for of such is the kingdom of heaven.”
Then Jesus laid his hands on them and left that place. The hindrance of wealth.
And a man came to Jesus and asked Him, “Teacher, what good deed must I do to inherit eternal life?”
And Jesus answered him, “Why do you ask me about what is good? There is only One who is good. But if you want to enter life, keep the commandments.”
And the man said, “What commandments?” And Jesus said, “‘Do not murder, do not commit adultery, do not steal.”
Do not bear false witness, honor your father and your mother.› #Do not kill… and your mother. 19:19 From the Book of Exodus 20: 12-16, and the Book of Deuteronomy 5: 16-20. And ‘love your neighbor #neighbor. 19:19 Referring to the Gospel of Luke 10: 25-37, we understand that the neighbor is e
So the young man said to him, “I have kept all these commandments; what do I still lack?”
And Jesus said to him, “If you want to be perfect, go and sell all your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come and follow me.”
When the young man heard that, he went away sad because he was very wealthy.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, “Truly I tell you, it is hard for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.”
And I tell you truly, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
And when His disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished and said, “Who then can be saved?”
And Jesus looked at them and said to them, “This is impossible with people, but with God all things are possible.”
Then Peter said to Him, “See, we have left all and followed You. What shall we have?”
Then Jesus said to them, “Truly I tell you, when the Son of Man sits on his glorious throne in the new age, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.”
And whoever leaves houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or sons or lands for My sake will receive a hundredfold and inherit eternal life.
Many are those among the people who will become the last of people, and many are those of the last of people who will become the first of people.
Like vineyard workers
And the kingdom of heaven is like a landowner who went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
And he agreed with the workers to pay them one dinar for each day, then he sent them to work in his vineyard.
And the generous man went out around nine o'clock, and saw some men standing in the market area doing nothing.
So he said to them, ‘Go and work in my vineyard as well, and I will give you what is right.’
So they went to work in the vineyard. “And he went out again about the twelfth hour, and about the third hour, and hired other workers.”
And around five o'clock he went out again and found others standing in the market area, so he asked them: "Why have you been standing all day without working?"
And they said to him, ‘No one has hired us.’ And he said to them, ‘You go and work in my vineyard too.’
And at the end of the day, the generous master said to his steward, ‘Call the laborers and pay them their wages, beginning with the last who was hired and ending with the first.’
Then those hired at five o'clock came and each received a denarius.
Then those who were hired first came and thought they would receive more, but each of them also received a denarius.
So they took it and began to complain about the generous man.
And they say, “Those who were hired last came in and worked only one hour, yet you paid them the same amount as us, who toiled in the heat all day!”
“Then the generous man said to one of them: ‘I have not wronged you, my friend! Didn’t you agree with me to work for one dinar?’”
Take your wage and go. I want to give the one I hired at the end of the day the same wage I gave you.
Don't I have the right to do what I want with what I own? Or are you jealous because I am kind to others?
Thus the first will be last, and the last first. Jesus foretells his death and resurrection.
And as Jesus was approaching Jerusalem, He took the twelve aside and said to them:
And now we are going to Jerusalem, where the Son of Man will be delivered to the chief priests and teachers of the law, and they will condemn him to death.
and they will hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and flogged and crucified. But on the third day he will rise from the dead.” The greatest is the one who serves.
Then the mother of Zebedee’s sons came to him with her sons, kneeling to ask him for something.
Then Jesus said to her, “What do you want?” And she said to Him, “Grant that these two sons of mine may sit, one at Your right hand and the other at Your left, in Your kingdom.”
Then Jesus said to them, “You do not know what you are asking. Are you able to drink the cup #cup. 22:20—the cup of suffering and affliction—that I am about to drink?” They said to Him, “We are able.”
He said to them, “As for my cup, you will drink from it; as for sitting at my right hand and at my left, I am not able to grant it except to those for whom my Father has prepared it.”
When the remaining ten heard this request, they became very angry with the two brothers.
Then Jesus called them to him and said, “You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their leaders exercise authority over them.
But this should not be among you. But whoever would be great among you must be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first among you must be your servant.
“So also the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.” Jesus Heals Two Blind Men.
And as they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd of people followed Him.
And there were two blind men sitting by the roadside. And when they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us.”
People rebuked them and told them to be quiet, but they raised their voices even more: “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us.”
So Jesus stopped and called to them, “What do you want me to do for you?”
And they said, “O Lord, open our eyes.”
And immediately Jesus’ sight was restored to them, and they saw, and followed Him.
The king lives.
As they approached Jerusalem and came to the village of Bethphage on the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two of his disciples,
And He said to them, “Go to the village that lies ahead. There you will find a young donkey tied with its mother. Untie them and bring them to Me.”
And if anyone says anything to you, tell him, “The Lord needs them, and He will bring them back soon.”
This happened so that what the Prophet said would be fulfilled:
Tell the beloved city Zion: #beloved Zion. 5:21 Literally “Daughter Zion.” ‹Behold, your king comes to you, humble, and mounted on a donkey, a young donkey, the foal of a beast of burden.›
So the two disciples went and did as Jesus had told them.
So they brought the young donkey and its mother and placed their clothes on it, and Jesus sat on the clothes.
And most people spread their cloaks on the road. But others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
And the crowds who were walking ahead of him and behind him were shouting: “Long live the King! #Long live the King. 9:21 Literally: “Hosanna.” And its meaning in Hebrew is: “Save now.” Most likely, here it is a cry of praise to God and his Christ the King. Repeated in verse 15. Long live the son of David. ‘Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.’ Long live the King on high.”
And when Jesus entered the city of Jerusalem, the whole city was shaken. And the people were asking, “Who is this man?”
And the crowds following him were saying, “This is Jesus the prophet, from the city of Nazareth, from Galilee.” Jesus in the temple.
Then Jesus entered the temple courts and drove out all those who were selling and buying there. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the benches of those selling doves.
And He said to them: “It is written, ‘My house shall be called a house of prayer’ #My house… prayer. 13:21 from the Book of Isaiah 56: 7. But you are making it a ‘den of thieves!’ #Den of thieves. 13:21 from the Book of Jeremiah 7: 11.”
And some who were blind and lame came to Him in the temple, and He healed them.
And the chief priests and the teachers of the law heard the miracles that he did, and saw the children crying in the temple, “Hosanna to the King! Blessed is the Son of David!” And they were indignant and said to him, “Do you hear what these are saying?”
And Jesus answered, “Yes, but have you not read in the Scriptures: ‘Out of the mouths of babies and infants, praise has been perfected’?”
Then Jesus left them and went out of the city to a town called Bethany, and spent the night there. The power of faith.
And as Jesus was going early in the morning into the city, He became hungry.
And he saw a fig tree by the roadside, so he went to it, but he found only leaves on it, and he said to it: “You will not produce fruit hereafter.” And the fig tree withered immediately.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed and asked him, “How did the fig tree wither so quickly?”
He answered them, “I tell you the truth, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will not only do what I did with the fig tree, but if you say to this mountain, ‘Be lifted up and thrown into the sea,’ it will happen.”
“And whatever you ask in prayer, you will receive if you have faith.” Questioning the authority of Jesus.
And Jesus went into the temple courts, and the chief priests and elders of the people came to him while he was teaching, and they said to him, “Tell us by what authority you are doing these things, and who gave you this authority?”
And Jesus answered them, “I will ask you also a question, and answer me, and I will tell you by what authority I do these things:
“Where did John’s baptism come from? From God or from human sources?” They began to discuss this among themselves and said, “If we say it came from God, he will ask us, ‘Why then did you not believe him?’”
And if we say it is from people, we fear the people, for they all regard John as a prophet.”
Therefore they answered Jesus and said, “We do not know.” And Jesus said to them, “Nor will I tell you by what authority I do these things.” The parable of the two sons.
And Jesus said: “What do you think about the following story: A man had two sons. He went to the first and said to him, ‘Son, go and work in my vineyard today.’”
The son replied, “I don’t want to go.” But he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to his other son and asked him the same thing. The son replied, “Yes, sir, I will go.” But he did not go.
“Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?” They said, “The first.” He said to them, “Truly I tell you, tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God before you.”
For John the Baptist came to show you the way of righteousness, and you did not believe him, but the tax collectors and prostitutes did. And even after you saw what he did, you did not repent or believe him. Like the son of a vineyard owner.
And listen to another parable: There was a man who had a vineyard, and he planted it and put a fence around it and dug a winepress in it and built a tower for the watchman. Then he leased it to some farmers and went away for a long time.
And when the time came to harvest the grapes, he sent his servants to the farmers to get his share of the grapes.
But the peasants seized his slaves, and beat one of them, and killed another, and stoned another.
So the owner sent more slaves than he had sent the first time. The farmers treated them in the same way.
And finally, he sent his son, and said to himself: ‹They will respect my son.›
But when the tenant farmers saw that he was the son of the owner, they conferred with one another and said, “This is the heir; let us kill him so that we may have his inheritance.”
So they seized him and threw him out of the vineyard and killed him.
So what do you think the generous master will do to those farmers when he returns?
And they said to him, “He will destroy them in a terrible way because they are wicked, then He will bestow kindness upon other farmers who give Him produce in the season of produce.”
And Jesus said to them, “Have you not read the scripture: ‘The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone; this was the Lord’s doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes’?”
Therefore I say to you, the kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a nation producing fruits worthy of the kingdom.
And whoever falls on this stone will be broken, and whoever this stone falls on will be crushed!
And when the chief priests and the Pharisees heard Jesus’ parables, they knew that He was speaking about them.
Therefore, they tried to arrest him, but they were afraid of the crowd because they considered Jesus a prophet.
like a wedding feast
And Jesus spoke to them again in parables, saying:
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who made a wedding feast for his son.
And the king sent his servants to summon the invited guests to the wedding feast, but they did not want to come.
So the king sent other servants, saying to them, ‘Tell those who were invited that the feast is ready. My oxen and the fattened cattle have been slaughtered, and everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast.’
But the invited guests did not care about the matter, and each of them went his own way. One went to work in his field, and another to his business.
As for the rest, they seized the king’s servants and beat them and killed them.
Then the king became angry and sent his army, and they killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants: ‘The wedding feast is ready, but those who were invited did not deserve it.’
Therefore, go out to the street corners and invite all whom you find to the wedding feast.
So they went out into the streets and gathered all those they found, whether wicked or righteous, until the banquet hall was filled with guests.
And when the king entered to see the guests, he saw a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
The king said to him, “O friend, how did you enter here without wearing wedding clothes?” But the man remained silent.
The king said to his servants: “Bind his feet and his hands, and cast him out into the darkness, where people weep and gnash their teeth.”
“Many are called, but few are chosen.” The Pharisees were trying to trap Jesus.
So the Pharisees went and plotted how they might ensnare Jesus in his words.
So they sent their disciples to him with people from the group of Herod, and said to him, “Teacher, we know that you are honest and teach the way of God truthfully. And that you do not show favoritism to anyone, for you do not regard people’s status.”
"So tell us your opinion, is it permissible to pay taxes to Caesar or not?"
But Jesus knew their wicked intent, and said to them, “Why do you try to trap me, you hypocrites?”
“Show me the coin you use.” So they brought him a denarius.
He said to them, “To whom does this drawing and this name inscribed on the dinar belong?”
Then they said to him, “It is Caesar’s.” He said to them, “Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and to God the things that are God’s.”
When they heard this answer, they were greatly astonished, and left Him and went their way. The Sadducees were trying to trap Jesus.
On that day, some Sadducees came to him, those who say there is no resurrection, and they asked him:
“O teacher,” Moses said—#Moses said. 24:22—See Deuteronomy 25:5, 6. For if a man dies without leaving a son, his brother shall marry his widow, and raise up offspring for his brother.
There were seven brothers among us, and the first married and died. Because he had no children, his brother married his widow.
And that happened to the brother, and the second, and the third, and even the seventh.
And when they were all dead, the woman died as well.
“To whom of the seven will she be wife on the Day of Resurrection? For they all married her.”
Jesus answered them, “You are mistaken, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God.”
Therefore understand that in eternal life after the resurrection of the dead, people neither marry nor are given in marriage, but are like angels in heaven.
As for the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what God said?
“I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob. #I… Jacob. 32:22 From the Book of Exodus 3:6. For God is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living.”
When the people heard this, they were greatly amazed at His teaching. The greatest commandment.
And when the Pharisees heard that Jesus had silenced the Sadducees, they gathered together.
And a legal expert questioned him, attempting to trap him, saying:
“Teacher, which is the greatest commandment in the Law?”
Then Jesus said to him, “‘You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind.’ #You shall love the Lord … your mind. 37:22 From Deuteronomy 6: 5.”
This is the first and greatest commandment.
And the second commandment is like the first: ‘Love your neighbor #صاحبك. 39:22 Referring to the Gospel of Luke 10:25-37, we understand that the neighbor is every person in need of help. Love him as yourself.’ #تحب صاحبك … نفسك. 39:22 From Leviticus 19:18.
“The whole Law and the Prophets are dependent on these two commandments.” Christ the Lord of David.
And while the Pharisees were gathered around Him, Jesus asked them:
“What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he?” The Pharisees answered, “He is the son of David.”
And he said to them, “If David calls him ‘Lord,’ how is he then his son?”
The Lord said to my lord: “Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.”
“If David calls Christ ‘Lord,’ how can he be his son?”
No one could answer him with anything, and no one dared to ask him further questions afterwards.
Jesus criticizes religious men.
Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said: “The teachers of the law and the Pharisees have left Moses in their interpretation of the law.”
So guard and practice everything they tell you, but do not do as they do. For they say, and do not act according to their words.
They burden people with difficult obligations, yet they themselves are unwilling to exert any effort to follow them.
All the good deeds they do, they do to be seen by people. They display their piety, and thus increase the size of their phylacteries #phylacteries. 5:23 Some Jews used to write specific passages from the Bible and place them in small leather pouches, then tie them with straps of cloth to their forehead or left arm, thereby showing their intense devotion, and lengthen the tassels of their garments.
They love to sit in the best seats at banquets, and in the front rows at gatherings.
And they like people to greet them with special greetings in the markets, and to call them: ‘O teacher.’
And do not be called teachers, for you have one Teacher, namely Christ, and you are all brothers.
And do not let anyone on earth call you ‘My Father,’ for you have one Father, who is in heaven.
And do not let people call you ‘Rabbi,’ for you have one Teacher, and he is the Messiah.
The greatest among you must be your servant.
And whoever exalts himself, God will abase him, and whoever humbles himself, God will exalt him.
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! You shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people’s faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor will you let those who are trying to enter go in.
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! For you devour widows’ houses and for the sake of appearance you make lengthy prayers. Therefore you will receive a more severe punishment.
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! For you travel through sea and land to win a single convert, and when you do, you make them twice as much a child of hell as you are.
Woe to you, blind guides, who say: ‘If anyone swears by the temple, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gold that is in the temple, he is bound!’
You foolish blind ones! Which is greater, the gold that is in the temple, or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
And you say, ‘If someone swears by the altar, he is not bound to keep his oath, but if someone swears by the gift that is on the altar, he is bound to keep it!’
My uncle! Which is greater: the offering that is on the altar, or the altar that makes the offering holy?
For if anyone swears by the altar, it is by the altar and all that is on it.
And if anyone swears by the Temple, he is swearing by it and by Him who dwells within it.
And if anyone swears by the heavens, he is swearing by the throne of God and by Him who is seated upon it.
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! You give a tenth of your income to the temple, even of mint and dill and cumin. #dill 23:23 A plant whose seeds were used in cooking as a spice, and it also has some medicinal uses. But you have neglected justice, mercy, and faithfulness. These are the things you should have done without neglecting the others.
You, deep guides, you strain at the gnat and swallow the camel!
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and dish, but inside they are full of greed and self-indulgence.
You Pharisees, blind guides, first clean the inside of the cup and the outside will also be clean.
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which appear beautiful on the outside, but on the inside are full of bones and impurity.
Thus you also appear righteous to others, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and wickedness.
Woe to you, Pharisees and teachers of the law, hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the monuments of the righteous.
And you say: ‹If we had lived in the days of our forefathers, we would not have participated in killing the prophets.›
And with this, you confirm that you are the offspring of those who killed the prophets.
So complete what your ancestors began.
You brood of vipers, offspring of snakes! How will you escape the judgment of hell?
Therefore, I tell you that I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. Some of them you will kill, some you will crucify, and some you will flog in your synagogues, and chase from city to city.
Therefore, you will be held accountable for the blood of every innocent person shed on earth, from the blood of innocent Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah. #Abel… Zechariah. 35:23 The first and last to be killed according to the time and text of the Old Testament. (See Genesis 4:8 and 2 Chronicles 24:20) Who was killed between the temple and the altar.
I tell you the truth, the punishment for all these crimes will fall on this generation. Jesus warns the people of Jerusalem.
O Jerusalem, O Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those sent by God to you! How often I have longed to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings! But you were unwilling.
Behold, your home will be left to you empty and deserted!
For I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, ‘Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.’ #Blessed… the Lord. 39:23 From Psalm 118:26.
Jesus Predicts the Destruction of the Temple
And Jesus left the temple grounds. As he was walking away, his disciples pointed out to him the temple buildings.
Then Jesus said to them, “Do you see all these buildings? Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on another; they will all be torn down!”
And as Jesus sat on the Mount of Olives, His disciples came to Him privately, saying, “Tell us, when will these things happen? And what will be the sign of Your coming and of the end of the age?”
And Jesus answered them, “Beware lest you be deceived.”
Many will come and claim my name, saying, ‘I am the Messiah,’ and they will deceive many.
You will hear news of wars and revolutions, so you should not be afraid. These things must happen, but they will not be the end of the world.”
For nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be earthquakes and famines,
But all these are still birth pangs.
They will hand you over to be punished, and they will kill some of you. And all nations will hate you because of my name.
At that time many will fall away, one will betray another to the authorities, and each will hate one another.
And false prophets will appear and deceive many.
And because of the increase of wickedness, the love of many will grow cold.
But whoever remains faithful until the end, this one will be saved.
And the gospel of the kingdom will be proclaimed throughout the whole world as a testimony to all nations, and then the end will come.
And when you see “the abomination that causes desolation” #the abomination that causes desolation. 15:24 See Daniel 9:27, 12:11, as well as Matthew 11:31. Which was indicated by the prophet Daniel, standing in the holy place – for the reader to understand this statement –
Let those, then, who are in Judea flee to the mountains,
And the one on the roof should not come down to take his possessions from the house.
And the worker in the field does not return to his home to take his cloak.
And how difficult were the conditions of pregnant and breastfeeding women in those days!
But pray that your flight does not take place in winter or on a Sabbath.
For it will be a time of great distress, unlike any that has come before or will ever come again.
And if God had not decreed to shorten those days, no one would remain alive. But for the sake of His chosen people, God will shorten those days.
If anyone tells you, ‘Look, here is the Messiah!’ or ‘There he is!’ do not believe it.
For many false messiahs will appear, and many false prophets. They will perform signs and wonders to deceive, if possible, even the elect.
Here I told you everything before it happened.
Someone might say, ‘Look, the Messiah is in the wilderness!’ Do not go there. Or they might say, ‘Look, he is in a private room!’ Do not believe them.
For as lightning comes from the east and shines to the west, so will the coming of the Son of Man be.
And wherever you find a corpse, you will find vultures as well.
Immediately after the distress of those days, ‘the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light. The stars will fall from the sky, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken.’
At that time, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the sky. And all the tribes of the earth will mourn, and they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of the sky with great power and glory.
And He will send His angels with a trumpet blast, and they will gather His chosen ones from the four corners of the earth, from the farthest reaches of the heavens.
Learn from the fig tree. As soon as its branches become tender and put forth their leaves, you know that summer is near.
And so also, when you see all these things, you will know that the time is near. #TheTime. 33:24 Compare with Luke 21:31. It is close at hand.
I tell you the truth, this generation will not pass away until all these things happen.
Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. The Father alone knows.
But no one knows the day or the hour, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but the Father alone knows.
And just as it was in the days of Noah, so will it be when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day Noah entered the ark.
They did not know what would happen, until the flood came and took them away. So it will be with the coming of the Son of Man.
At that time, two men will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
And there were two women grinding grain on a millstone, one was taken and the other left.
Be vigilant, therefore, for you do not know when your Lord will come.
Make sure that if the homeowner knew what time of night the thief intended to come, he would have woken up and not let him burgle his house.
Therefore, be ready yourselves, for the Son of Man will come at an hour you do not expect. The faithful servant and the wicked servant.
So who is the faithful and prudent servant whom his master will put in charge of his household, to give them their portion of food at the proper time?
Blessed is that servant whom his lord finds so doing when he comes.
I tell you the truth, he will entrust all his possessions to him.
But the wicked servant says to himself, ‘My master will be slow to come.’
So he begins by striking his fellow servants, and begins to eat and drink with the drunkards.
Then the master of that servant will come on a day he does not expect, and at an hour he does not know.
He will be punished and placed with the hypocrites, where people weep and gnash their teeth.
Like ten girls.
Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them are foolish, and five are intelligent.
So the foolish ones took their lamps, but did not bring extra oil with them.
Now as for the wise ones, they took extra oil in their jars along with the lamps.
So the groom was late, and all the girls grew drowsy and fell asleep.
But at midnight, a voice cried out: ‘Here comes the bridegroom, come out to meet him!’
Then the girls woke up and prepared their lamps.
And the foolish ones said to the wise ones: “Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out.”
And the intelligent ones replied: “We cannot, it is not enough for us, but go to the merchants to buy oil yourselves.”
And as they went to buy oil, the bridegroom came. And the wise were ready, so they went in with him to the wedding feast. Then the door was shut.
And finally the rest of the girls came and said: “Sir, sir, open the door for us.”
But he said, ‘Truly I tell you, I do not know you!’
Therefore be alert, for you do not know on what day your Lord will come. Like the three slaves.
“Also, the kingdom of heaven is like a man going on a journey. He called his servants and entrusted them with his possessions.”
So he gave one of them five bags #five bags. 15:25 Literally “manas or qanāṭīr,” and the manna equals about 27 to 36 kilograms of gold, silver, or copper. Of money, and he gave the second two bags, and the third one bag. He gave to each according to his ability. Then he traveled immediately afterward.
So the one who took the five bags immediately invested them in trade, and earned five more bags.
And the one who took the two bags did as the first, and won two more bags.
Now, the one who took a single bag went and dug a hole in the ground, and hid his master’s money in it.
And after a long time, the master of these slaves returned and began to settle accounts with them.
Then came the one who had taken the five bags, and he gained five more bags and said: ‘My lord, you gave me five bags, and here are five more bags I have earned.’
So his master said to him, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful over a little, I will entrust you with much. Enter and share in your master’s joy.’
Then the one who had taken the two bags came and said, “My lord, you gave me two bags, and these are two other bags I have earned.”
So his master said to him, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful over a little, I will entrust you with much. Enter and share in your master’s joy.’
Then came the one who had taken a single bag and said: “Sir, I know that you are a hard man, reaping where you have not sown, and gathering where you have not scattered seed.”
And I was afraid of you, so I went and buried your bag in the ground. Take your money.
“His master replied to him: ‘You are a wicked and lazy servant. Since you know that I reap from a harvest I did not sow, and gather from fields I did not seed,’"
Why didn't you deposit my money in the bank, so that when I returned, I could take my money with interest?
Therefore, take the bag from him and give it to the owner of the ten bags.
For it will be given to him who has, and he will have more; but from him who does not have, even what he has will be taken away.
As for that useless servant, throw him outside into the darkness, where people weep and gnash their teeth. The Son of Man is the judge.
And when the Son of Man comes in his glory with all the angels, then he will sit on his glorious throne.
Then all nations will be gathered before him, and he will separate them from one another, as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.
He will put the sheep on his right, and the goats on his left.
Then the King will say to those on his right hand, ‘Come, you who are blessed by my Father. Take possession of the kingdom prepared for you since the foundation of the world.’
For I was hungry and you gave me food. I was thirsty and you gave me drink. I was a stranger and you welcomed me.
I was naked and you clothed me. I was sick and you cared for me. I was imprisoned and you visited me.
And the righteous will answer him, ‘Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you drink?
And when did we see you as a stranger and give you shelter, or naked and clothe you?
And when We saw you sick or imprisoned, We visited you.
Then the king will say to them, ‘Truly I tell you, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did for me.’"
Then the king will say to those standing on his left: ‘Depart from me, you cursed, and go into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.’
Because I was hungry, you did not feed me. I was thirsty, and you did not give me drink.
I was a stranger and you did not welcome me. I was naked and you did not clothe me. I was sick and imprisoned and you did not visit me.
Then the wicked will reply, ‘Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not minister to you?’
Then the king will say to them, ‘Truly I tell you, whatever you did not do for one of the least of these brothers and sisters of mine, you did not do for me.’
And so the wicked go to eternal punishment, but the righteous go to eternal life.
Jewish leaders are plotting to kill Jesus.
After Jesus had finished saying these things, he said to his disciples:
You know that Easter is after tomorrow, and the Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of his enemies to be crucified.
The chief priests and elders of the people had gathered in the palace of the high priest Caiaphas.
and they plotted to arrest Jesus by deceit and to kill him.
And they were saying, “It is not right to do this during the festival, to avoid causing a disturbance among the people.” A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
And while Jesus was in Bethany at the house of Simon the leper,
A woman came to him with an expensive jar of perfume made of pure nard. She broke the jar and poured the perfume on his head while he was reclining at the table.
And when His disciples saw it, they were indignant and said, “Why this waste?”
This perfume could have been sold for a large sum of money, given to the poor.
So Jesus knew what they were discussing with one another, and he said to them, “Why do you trouble this woman? She has done a beautiful thing for me.”
The poor will always be with you, #thepoor … always. 11:26 See Deuteronomy 15:11. But I will not be with you always.
I poured perfume on my body in preparation for burial.
“I tell you the truth, wherever this gospel is preached throughout the world, what she has done will also be told, as a memorial to her.” Judas Iscariot betrays Jesus.
Then one of the twelve, whose name was Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests,
And he said to them, “What will you give me if I deliver Jesus to you?” So they offered him thirty pieces of silver.
From that moment, Judas began to look for a suitable opportunity to hand Jesus over to them. At the Passover meal.
And on the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread, when the disciples came to Him, they said, “Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?”
And Jesus said: “Go to so-and-so in the village, and tell him: ‘The Teacher says: My appointed time is near, and I will celebrate the Passover with my disciples in your house.’”
So the disciples did as Jesus had told them, and prepared the Passover supper.
And when evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with his twelve disciples.
And while they were eating, He said, “Truly I say to you, one of you will betray Me.”
And they became sorrowful and began to ask Him one after another, “Is it I, Lord?”
And Jesus answered, “It is he who has dipped his hand with me in the dish who will betray me.”
“For the Son of Man must go, as it is written of him, but woe to that man who betrays the Son of Man! It would have been better for him if he had not been born.”
Then Judas, who would betray him, asked, “Is it I, Rabbi?” He said to him, “You have said so yourself!” The Last Supper
And while they were eating, He took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to the disciples, saying, “Take, eat; this is My body.”
Then he took a cup of wine, gave thanks, and gave it to them, saying, “Drink from this, all of you.”
For this is my blood, the blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.
And I tell you that I will not drink of this fruit of the vine until the day I drink it new with you in my Father’s kingdom.”
Then they sang hymns and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus predicts Peter’s denial.
Then Jesus said to them, “All of you will be made to stumble because of me tonight. For it is written: ‘I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.’"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go before you to Galilee.”
Then Peter replied, “Even if everyone falls away because of you, I will never fall away.”
Then Jesus said to him, “Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows, you will disown me three times.”
Then Peter said to him, “Even if I have to die with you, I will not deny you!” And all the disciples said the same. Jesus prays in solitude.
Then Jesus went with them to a place called Gethsemane, and said to his disciples, “Sit here while I go over there to pray.”
And he took with him Peter and the sons of Zebedee, and began to be sorrowful and very heavy.
Then he said to them, “My sorrow is very great, so much so that it almost kills me! Stay here and keep watch with me.”
And Jesus withdrew a little from them and fell on his face and prayed, saying, “My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. #TheCup. 26:39 That is, the cup of pain and suffering. Repeated in verse 42. But not as I will, but as you will.”
And he came to his disciples and found them sleeping, and said to Peter, “Could you not watch with me for one hour?”
Strive and pray so you do not experience it. Your spirit seeks it, but your body is weak.
And he stepped away to pray, then said: “My father, if this cup cannot be taken from me, but I must drink it, may your will be done.”
Then he returned a second time and found them sleeping, for their eyes were heavy with sleep.
So he left them and went to pray a third time, saying the same words he had said before.
Then he returned to his disciples and said to them: “Are you still sleeping and resting? Look, the hour is near, and the Son of Man is about to be handed over to the hands of sinners.”
Get up and let's go. Here comes the man who betrayed me.” The arrest of Jesus.
And while he was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, appeared, accompanied by a large crowd armed with swords and clubs, who had been sent by the chief priests and the elders of the people.
And the betrayer had given them a sign, saying, “The one I kiss is the man; seize him.”
So he came close to Jesus and said to Him, “Greetings, Teacher!” and kissed Him.
Then Jesus said to him, “Friend, do what you came for.” At that, they approached and seized Jesus and arrested him.
Then one of those who were with Jesus stretched out his hand and drew his sword and struck the servant of the high priest and cut off his ear.
Then Jesus said to him, “Put your sword back in its place. For all who take up the sword will perish by the sword.”
Do you not realize that I could call upon my Father, and He would immediately send more than twelve legions of angels?
But if you did, how would the books you declared would foretell these things in this way be verified?
At that moment Jesus said to the crowds: “Did you come out with swords and clubs as you would against a criminal? Every day I sat in the temple courts teaching, and you did not arrest me!”
But this happened so that what was written by the prophets might be fulfilled.” Then all the disciples deserted him and fled! Jesus before the Jewish leaders.
Then those who had arrested him led him to the house of Caiaphas the high priest, where the teachers of the law and the elders had gathered.
But Peter followed him from a distance and went into the courtyard of the high priest. He went in and sat with the guards to see how it would end.
Now the chief priests and all the council were seeking testimony against Jesus to put him to death.
But they did not find [anyone], although many false witnesses came forward and spoke lies about Him. Finally, two men came forward,
And they said: "This man #This man. 61:26, meaning Jesus, for his enemies avoided uttering his name! He said: 'I can tear down the temple of God and rebuild it in three days.'"
So the high priest stood up and said, “Will you not answer the accusations these two men are making against you?”
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, “I adjure you by the living God to tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God.”
Then Jesus answered him, “Yes, I am. And I say to you, from now on you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of God and coming on the clouds of heaven.”
At that, the high priest tore his clothes and said, “You have heard the blasphemy. What need have we any longer of witnesses? You have all heard it yourselves.”
“By what standard do you judge?” They replied, “He deserves death.”
Then they spat in his face, and struck him with their fists and slapped him.
And they were saying to him, “Prophesy, Messiah, who struck you?” Peter denies Jesus.
Meanwhile, Peter was sitting in the courtyard outside. Then a servant girl of the high priest came and said to him, “You also were with Jesus the Galilean.”
But Peter denied this before them all, saying, “I do not understand what you are saying!”
Then he went out to the gate of the courtyard, and another servant girl said to those who were there, “This man was with Jesus of Nazareth.”
Then Peter denied it again, and swore, saying, “I do not know this man.”
And after a little while, those standing there came to him and said, “You are indeed one of them, for your accent reveals that you are Galilean.”
Then he began to curse—#curse. 74:26—that is, to swear upon himself with a curse if he were lying! And he swore and said, “I do not know this man.” And immediately the rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered the word that Jesus had spoken: “Before the rooster crows, you will disown me three times.” And he went outside and wept bitterly.
Jesus before Pontius Pilate
And in the morning of the following day, the chief priests and elders of the people gathered together and conferred in order to kill Jesus.
They seized him and led him away, and handed him over to Pilate the governor. And Judas went and hanged himself.
When Judas, who betrayed Jesus, saw that they had decided to condemn Jesus to death, he regretted what he had done. So he returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders.
And he said to them, “I have erred in handing over an innocent person to be killed.” They said to him, “What concern is this of ours? Sort this matter out yourself.”
Then Judas threw the pieces of silver into the temple and departed, and went and hanged himself.
So the chief priests took the silver and said, “It is not right to put this money into the temple treasury, since it is blood money.”
So they decided to buy the potter’s field to use as a burial place for foreigners.
And for this reason, the field is known as the “Field of Blood” to this day.
And thus was fulfilled what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet Jeremiah: “They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price set on the man by the people of Israel.”
“and they bought the field of the potter, as the Lord commanded me.” #They took… the Lord. 10:27 See the Book of Zechariah 11:12-13, and the Book of Jeremiah 32:6-9. Jesus before Pilate
And Jesus stood before the governor, and the governor asked Him, “Are You the King of the Jews?” And Jesus said to him, “You say so yourself.”
And when the chief priests and elders accused him, he answered nothing.
Then Pilate asked him, “Don’t you hear all the accusations they are making against you?”
But Jesus gave Pilate no answer to any of the charges they made against him. Pilate was greatly amazed at his silence. Pilate failed to release Jesus.
The governor used to customarily release a prisoner chosen by the people for Easter.
And there was a notorious prisoner named Barabbas. #Barabbas. 16:27 or “Jesus Barabbas” as in some Greek manuscripts.
And when the crowd gathered, Pilate asked them, “Whom do you want me to release to you: Jesus, who is called Christ, or Barabbas?”
Now Pilate knew that they had delivered Him to him out of envy.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message saying, “Do not do anything to this innocent man, for I have suffered much in a dream about him.”
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas’ release and to have Jesus put to death.
Then the governor asked them, “Which of the two do you want me to release to you?” And they said, “Barabbas.”
So Pilate asked them, “What shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?” And they all answered, “Crucify Him.”
Then Pilate asked them, “Why? What crime has he committed?” But they shouted all the more, “Crucify him!”
When Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere, but that the crowd was becoming more and more agitated, he took some water and washed his hands in front of them, saying, “I am innocent of this man’s blood. It is your responsibility.”
Then all the people said, “His blood be on us and on our children.”
Then Pilate released Barabbas to them, and ordered Jesus to be flogged, and handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
Then the governor’s soldiers took Jesus to the Praetorium and gathered a whole company of soldiers around him.
So they stripped him of his clothes and dressed him in a crimson robe. #They dressed him… in color. 28:27 Or purple, and they mocked him, for this is the color of a king’s robe.
And they wove a crown of thorny branches and placed it on his head, and they put a reed in his right hand, and they prostrated themselves before him, mocking him, and saying, “Long live the King of the Jews!”
Then they spat on him, took the staff from his hand, and began to strike him on the head.
And when they had mocked him, they took off his garment, and put his own clothes on him, and led him out to crucify him. Jesus on the cross.
And when they led Him out, they pressed into service a man from Cyrene, named Simon, to bear His cross.
And when they arrived at a place called “Golgotha,” which means “Place of the Skull,”
They gave Jesus wine mixed with a bitter substance to drink. When he tasted it, he refused to drink.
And when they crucified Jesus, they divided his garments among them, and cast lots for them.
Then they sat there guarding him.
And they posted above his head a sign with the inscription: “This is Jesus, the King of the Jews,” as his charge.
And two criminals were crucified with Him, one on His right and the other on His left.
And the passersby were cursing him and shaking their heads.
And they said, “You who would destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days, if you are the Son of God, save yourself, and come down from the cross!”
And so the chief priests, teachers of the law and elders mocked him, saying:
Let him save others, but he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now, and we will believe in him!
He put his trust in God. Let God rescue him now if he wants him. Didn’t he say, ‘I am the Son of God’?”
And the criminals who were crucified with Him were also insulting Him with similar words. The death of Jesus.
And from the twelfth hour noon, darkness came over all the land until the third hour in the afternoon.
And about the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, “Eli, Eli, lema sabachthani?” #Eli… sabachthani. 46:27 From Psalm 22:1. That is: “My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?”
When some of those standing there heard this, they said, “It is Elijah calling!” #Elijah calling. 47:27 The word “Eli” is Hebrew and “Elo” is Aramaic, resembling the name “Elijah,” which is the name of a well-known prophet who lived around 850 BC.
Then one of those standing there hurried forward, took a sponge, dipped it in vinegar, placed it on a long reed, and offered it to him to drink.
As for the others, they were saying, “Let us wait and see if Elijah will come to save him!”
Then Jesus cried out again with a loud voice, and breathed his last.
The veil of the temple split. #templeveil. 51:27 The veil that separated the “Holy of Holies” from the rest of the Jewish temple. And the Holy of Holies represented the divine presence. It split in two from top to bottom, the earth shook, and the rocks split.
And the tombs were opened, and many bodies of the saints who had fallen asleep were raised to life.
And after the resurrection of Jesus, those bodies came out of the tombs and entered the holy city, and appeared to many.
Now, the Roman officer, #Roman officer. 54:27 literally “commander of a hundred.” And the guards who were guarding Jesus’ body, when they saw the earthquake and the other events, were very frightened and said, “Truly this was the Son of God!”
There were also women looking on from afar, who had followed Jesus from Galilee to minister to Him.
And among them were Mary Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee. #sons of Zebedee. 56:27 James and John. They buried Jesus.
And when evening came, a rich man from the town of Ramah named Joseph came to Jerusalem. He was also a disciple of Jesus.
So he went to Pilate and asked him for the body of Jesus. Pilate gave orders to hand it over.
So Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth,
Then he placed him in his new tomb that he had hewn in the rock, then rolled a large stone to the entrance of the tomb and went away.
And Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting opposite the tomb, watching over Jesus’ tomb.
And on the next day, after the Sabbath, the chief priests and Pharisees gathered together with Pilate,
And they said to Him, “Rabbi, we remember that while He was still alive, He said, ‘I will rise from the dead after three days.’”
“Therefore, give orders to guard the tomb until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples may come and steal the body and tell the people, ‘He has risen from the dead.’ This deception would be worse than the first.”
Then Pilate said to them, “Take a guard of soldiers, go and make sure of everything for yourselves.”
So they went and secured the tomb. They placed a seal on the stone, and they stationed guards from the soldiers on it.
Resurrection of Jesus
And after the Sabbath, on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary came to the tomb.
And at that moment a strong earthquake occurred, because an angel of the Lord descended from heaven and went to the tomb and rolled away the stone from the door, then sat on it.
His appearance was radiant like lightning, and his clothes were white as snow.
The guards were very afraid of him and became like the dead.
And the angel said to the women, “Do not be afraid, for I know that you seek Jesus who was crucified.”
He is not here, for He has risen as He previously said. Come and see the place where He was lying.
Then go quickly to his disciples and tell them, ‘He has risen from the dead, and he will go before you to Galilee. There you will see him.’ ‘I have told you,’ he said.”
Then the two women left the tomb quickly, their fear mingled with great joy, and ran to tell the disciples what had happened.
And suddenly Jesus met them and said, “Peace.” They approached Him, grasped His feet, and worshiped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, “Do not be afraid; go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, where they will see me.” The false report.
And while the two women were on their way, some of the guards went into the city and told the chief priests everything that had happened.
So the chief priests and elders gathered together, and conferred with one another, then they gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
And they told them, “Spread among the people that the disciples of Jesus came by night and stole His body while you were sleeping.”
“And if this news reaches the governor, we will convince him and keep you safe.”
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were told. And this story spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus speaks to his disciples.
And the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go.
And when they saw him, they prostrated to him, although some of them had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.”
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,
And teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And remember, I am with you always, to the end of the age.”
John the Baptist
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
As it is written in the book of the prophet Isaiah: “Behold, I send my messenger before your face, to prepare your way.”
A voice of a man crying in the wilderness, saying, “Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.”
John the Baptist came, baptizing in the wilderness, and proclaiming that people should be baptized as a demonstration of their repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
And all the inhabitants of the villages of the region of Judea and the city of Jerusalem came out to him and were baptized by him in the Jordan River, confessing their sins.
His clothes were made of camel hair, he wore a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey.
And he was proclaiming and saying: “A man greater than I will come after me, and I am not worthy to bend down and untie his shoelaces.”
“I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit.” The Baptism of Jesus.
And in those days Jesus came from Nazareth, a town in Galilee, and was baptized by John in the Jordan River.
And as He came up out of the water, He saw the heavens opening, and the Holy Spirit descending upon Him like a dove.
And a voice came from heaven: “This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.” The baptism of Jesus.
And the Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness alone.
And he remained there for forty days, facing the temptations of the devil. He was with the wild animals, and angels were ministering to him. Jesus chooses some of his disciples.
And after John was arrested, Jesus came into Galilee, proclaiming the gospel of God.
And He says: “The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent and believe in the gospel.”
And as he walked by the Sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and his brother Andrew casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
Then Jesus said to them, “Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.”
So they left their net behind immediately and followed him.
Then he went on a little further and saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John in their boat, mending their nets.
So Jesus called to them, and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men and followed him. Jesus casts out an unclean spirit.
Then they went to Capernaum, and Jesus entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and began to teach.
And they were amazed by his teaching, for he taught them with authority, and not like the teachers of the law.
And in the synagogue there was a man possessed by an unclean spirit, and he cried out:
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are—the Holy One of God.
Jesus rebuked it and said to it, “Be silent and come out of him!”
Then the unclean spirit entered the man and threw him into convulsions, and after crying out with a loud voice, it came out of him.
And so everyone was astonished, and they began to say to one another, “What is this new teaching? For He commands unclean spirits with authority, and they obey Him.”
And the news about him spread quickly throughout the Galilee region. Jesus healed many.
Then they departed from the synagogue and went with James and John to the home of Simon and Andrew.
And Simon’s mother-in-law was in bed with a fever. They told Jesus about her.
So he approached her, took her hand, and seated her. The fever left her, and she began to serve them.
And that evening, at sunset, they brought to Him many who were sick and those possessed by demons.
Then all the people of the city gathered at the door of the house.
And Jesus healed many who were sick with various diseases, and cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak because they knew who he was. Preparing to proclaim the gospel.
And very early in the morning, before sunrise, Jesus went out alone and went to a secluded place to pray.
So Simon and those who were with him went out to search for him.
And when they found him, they said to him, “Everyone is looking for you!”
And Jesus answered them, “Let us go to the neighboring villages so that I may preach there also, for that is why I came.”
So he went throughout all the region of Galilee, preaching in their synagogues, and casting out demons. Jesus heals a leper.
And a man covered in leprosy came to Jesus, kneeling and begging him, saying, “Lord, you have the power to heal me, if you want.”
And Jesus, moved with compassion, stretched out His hand and touched him, saying, “I will; be clean.”
So the leprosy left the man, and he became clean.
Then Jesus rebuked him sternly before dismissing him.
And he said to him, “Beware of telling anyone what happened to you, but go and show yourself to the priest, #go… to the priest. 44:1 The priest was the one who decided, according to the law, when a leper was considered clean. And offer the offering for your purification as Moses commanded,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,ევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევევ
But the man went off and began to spread news of his healing everywhere, so it became difficult for Jesus to enter openly into any town, but He would stay in remote places, and people were coming to Him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a paralyzed man.
And after some days, Jesus returned to Capernaum, and news of His return spread.
So a great crowd gathered that there was no longer room for anyone, not even outside the door. And Jesus was speaking to the people, teaching them about God.
So they brought to Him a paralyzed man, carried by four men.
But they could not get him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they removed the roof above where Jesus was, and when they had made an opening, they lowered the bed on which the paralytic was lying.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralytic, “Son, your sins are forgiven.”
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there, thinking to themselves:
Why does this man speak this way? He is insulting God with his words! For who but God alone can forgive sins?
And Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said to them, “Why are you thinking this in your hearts?”
Which is easier: to say to the paralyzed man, ‘Your sins are forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Get up, take your mat, and walk’?
“But that you may know that the Son of Man has power on earth to forgive sins”—He said to the man who was paralyzed,
I tell you, rise, take up your bed, and go to your home!
So he rose and immediately picked up his bed and walked in the sight of all; and they were all amazed and glorified God, and said, “We have never seen anything like this!” Levi (Matthew) follows Jesus.
And Jesus went again to the lake. And He was teaching the crowds who followed Him there.
And as he walked, he saw Levi sitting at the tax collector’s booth. “Follow me,” he said to him, and Levi got up and followed him.
While Jesus was sitting in Levi’s house eating dinner, many tax collectors and sinners were eating with him and his disciples. For there were many who followed Jesus when they heard that he had called Levi, and were eating with him.
When the Pharisees and the teachers of the law saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, they asked his disciples, “Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?”
When Jesus heard this, he said to them, “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners.” A question about fasting.
Now the disciples of John and of the Pharisees were fasting, and people came to Jesus and asked him, “Why do the disciples of John and of the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not?”
Then Jesus said to them, “How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.”
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth, for the new piece will pull away and tear the old garment, and the tear will become worse.
And no one puts new wine into old wineskins, for the wine will burst the skins and spill, and the skins will be ruined. Therefore, new wine must be put into new wineskins.” Jesus: Lord of the Sabbath
And on a Sabbath Jesus was passing through the fields, and his disciples began to pluck ears of corn as they went.
Then the Pharisees said to Jesus, “Look! Your disciples are doing what is not lawful on the Sabbath!”
He said to them, “Have you never read in the Book what David did when he and his companions were hungry?”
He entered the house of God in the time of the priest Abiathar and ate the loaves of bread presented to God, and gave some to those who were with him. But no one is permitted to eat that bread exc
Then Jesus said to them, “The Sabbath was made for mankind, and not mankind for the Sabbath.”
Thus, the Son of Man is also Lord of the Sabbath.”
Jesus heals on the Sabbath
And Jesus went again into the synagogue, and there was a man there with a withered hand.
Some people were watching him closely, to see if he would recover, to find a reason to accuse him.
Then He said to the man with the paralyzed hand, “Get up and come forward!”
Then He said to them, “Is it permissible to do good or to do evil on the Sabbath? Is it permissible to save a life or to take it?” And they remained silent.
And Jesus looked around at them with anger, and was grieved at the hardness of their hearts. Then He said to the man, “Stretch out your hand,” and he stretched it out, and it was restored.
So the Pharisees went and conspired with the followers of Herod how to kill Jesus. Many followed Jesus.
And Jesus went with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee, and a great crowd from Galilee and Judea followed him.
And from Jerusalem and Idumea and beyond the Jordan, and from the region around Tyre and Sidon, a great multitude came to him, having heard of the deeds he was doing.
So Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a boat for him, so that the crowds would not press on him.
Since Jesus had healed many, everyone who suffered from any illness tried to get close to Him to touch Him.
And the unclean spirits fell before him, crying out, “You are the Son of God!”
He strongly warns her not to reveal who he is. The choice of twelve.
Then Jesus went up the mountain and called to him those whom he desired, and they went with him.
And Jesus chose twelve men and called them apostles, to be with Him, and to send them out to various places.
and He gives them authority to cast out unclean spirits.
Illusion: Simeon, whom he named Peter,
James son of Zebedee and his brother John, whom Jesus called “Boanerges”—that is, “sons of thunder.”
Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot, #theZealot. 18:3 From an Aramaic word meaning "zealous." That is, belonging to a Jewish political party resisting Roman rule, called the "Zealots."
And Judas Iscariot, who betrayed him. Jesus demonstrated the power of God and Jesus returned to the house.
And the people gathered around him and around his disciples again, so much so that they did not even have time to eat.
When Jesus’ family heard about what he was doing, they came to get him, believing he was out of his mind!
Now the Pharisees and teachers of the law who came from Jerusalem said, “He drives out demons by the power of Beelzebul, #Beelzebul. 22:3 A name for Satan. And by the power of the prince of demons he casts out demons.”
Then Jesus called them and began to speak to them in parables: “How can Satan cast out a demon?
For if a kingdom is divided against itself, it cannot stand.
And a house divided against itself cannot stand.
